# Fault source for the backend leg of stateful-property-fuzz. Every generated
# step the driver routes to /mc (the `backend` step kind) exercises one
# occurrence of the memcached `get` command against this fake, and the fault
# stanzas below are keyed to those occurrences in order -- get:1 is the FIRST
# backend-routed step, get:2 the second, and so on. Copied from
# property-fuzz/backend (which mirrors backend-rst-midreply/backend and
# backend-lying-length/backend).
#
# Sized generously (40 stanzas) for a checked-in seed that produces roughly a
# sixth of NUM_STEPS as backend steps (fate==0 of six): the generator's branch
# is a property of the PRNG stream, not something this file can predict exactly,
# so there is headroom rather than an exact count. A fault whose occurrence
# number the run never reaches is inert -- unused fault stanzas cost nothing --
# so headroom is free and an exact count is not worth chasing (and would
# silently go stale the day the seed, the corpus size, or the branch
# probability changes).
#
# WHY 40 STANZAS (headroom, not replay). Unlike property-fuzz, this scenario has
# NO prober-TAP replay pass: its determinism/replay guarantee is PLAN-LEVEL (the
# step plan regenerates byte-identically from the seed), and the lifecycle
# events (kill respawn pids, phase-shifted fakesrv counter) make byte-identical
# TAP replay impossible on purpose (see driver.sh's header). So 40 is pure
# headroom for the single forward walk of the plan -- the fakesrv is one process
# whose get:N counter runs over the whole scenario lifetime, and 40 stanzas
# comfortably covers the backend steps a NUM_STEPS=32 plan can draw with the
# rotation-of-four fault shapes intact. It is NOT sized for a second replay run.
#
# Four actions rotate across the occurrences, each a distinct upstream-failure
# shape that unwinds through ngx_http_upstream_finalize_request differently:
#   truncate      -- correct reply cut mid-frame, then RST (earliest possible
#                     upstream death: the client has parsed nothing usable)
#   rst           -- reset with nothing sent at all
#   accept_close  -- accepted then closed before a byte is read either way
#   lie_bytes     -- declared length disagrees with the payload (the classic
#                     reply-framing off-by-one, in both directions)
# None of these differ per key, so every seeded value is the same shape; what
# varies is how CORRECTLY it dies, not what it contains.
